ST.append(U(SPU, 'Wagons (with Defenders)', [M('Wagon', '- - - - 6 3 - - -', 15, mn=1, mx=5, key='wagon'), M('Wagon Defender', '4 2 3 3 3 1 2 1 7', 4, mn=0, mx=25, key='defender', weapons=['Hand Weapon', 'Javelin'], rules=['Skirmishers (Byzantium)'], options=[('Shield', 1), ('Bow (replaces javelins)', 1), ('Crossbow (Cumans; every other model)', 5)])],
    'Each Wagon (15 pts) may have up to five defenders with a hand weapon and javelins (4 pts each). Wagon Defenders may have shield (+1 pt), and exchange javelins for a bow (+1 pt). Cuman Wagon Defenders: every other Wagon Defender model may be equipped with a crossbow (+5 pts per model). Wagon Deployment: the Wagons are deployed as one unit and must be placed on the table as the first unit, 3" apart in a circle or semi-circle, with two Wagons at each end touching the Nomad player\'s table edge. The Wagons do not block line of sight. Defender Deployment: all the Defenders are Skirmishers, they count as one unit and may not take a Standard or a Musician. They are placed inside the Wagon circle and may not leave voluntarily. As long as they remain inside the Wagon circle they count as being behind hard cover. The Wagons count as a defended obstacle until the defenders lose a round of close combat. Victory Points: for each undefended Wagon at the end of the game the opponent gains an additional +15 Victory points. 0-5 Wagons. Pechenegs and Cumans.', page='60'))
ST.append(U(SPU, 'Yurts (with Defenders)', [M('Yurt', '- - - - 5 2 - - -', 5, mn=3, mx=5, key='yurt'), M('Yurt Defender', '4 2 3 3 3 1 2 1 7', 4, mn=0, mx=20, key='defender', weapons=['Hand Weapon', 'Javelin'], rules=['Skirmishers (Byzantium)'], options=[('Shield', 1), ('Bow (replaces javelins)', 1)])],
    'Each Yurt (5 pts) may have up to four Defenders with a hand weapon and javelins (4 pts each). Yurt Defenders may have shield (+1 pt), and exchange javelins for a bow (+1 pt). Yurt Deployment: the whole Yurt Camp is deployed as one unit and must be placed on the table as the first unit, with the Yurts 3" apart in a circle or semi-circle, with at least one Yurt touching the Nomad player\'s table edge. The Yurts do not block line of sight. Defender Deployment: all the defenders are Skirmishers, they count as one unit and may not take a Standard or a Musician. They are placed behind the Yurts and may not leave voluntarily. As long as they remain inside the Yurt Camp they count as being behind soft cover. Victory Points: for each undefended Yurt at the end of the game the opponent gains an additional +5 Victory points. 3-5 Yurts. Avars, Khazars and Magyars.', page='60'))
ST.append(U(SPU, 'Beg War Wagon', [M('War Wagon', '- - - - 7 3 - - -', 55, key='wagon'), M('Wagon Guards', '4 3 3 3 3 1 3 1 7', 8, mn=0, mx=4, key='guards', weapons=['Hand Weapon', 'Light Armour', 'Shield'])],
    'The War Wagon may have up to four guards armed with a hand weapon, light armour and a shield at the cost of +8 pts per model. The War Wagon is immobile and counts as a large target, if targeted specifically. Alternatively, the models inside the War Wagon can be targeted - they count as being behind hard cover, and use the wagon as a defended obstacle in close combat. All fleeing Khazar units that come within 6" of the War Wagon may immediately take a Rally test, even if in the middle of a flight. If the test is passed, the unit rallies and may reform. Units that were pursued and caught but who managed to rally do not count as destroyed - the pursuing unit counts as charging in the next turn of close combat. Later Khazars only; Special Units may be either Yurts & Defenders, or the Beg War Wagon. Only if a Beg (Khagan or Great Khagan) is the Army General.', mx=1, page='57'))
STV = variants('ST', 'Steppe Nomads', {
 AV: (L(Characters=25, Special_Units=25, **{'Allies_and_Mercenaries': (25, 100)}), 'AVARS (558-631 AD). Characters up to 25%; Cavalry any amount; Infantry none; Special Units up to 25% (Yurts and Defenders); Subjects at least 25% of the points from Infantry and cavalry of the Early Bulgars and Early Balkan Slavs lists (the Subject Tribesmen here; Slav and Bulgar units from the Bulgars & Slavs list are documented only). Two units of Gepid Cavalry may also be chosen (Germanic Kingdoms list). Allies: one Lombard unit may be taken if no Gepids have been taken. Avar Nobles equipment: hand weapon and bow. May have light armour (+1 pt), heavy armour (+2 pts), buckler (+1 pt), half-barding (+2 pts), and Kontarion & shield (+3 pts). Avar Horse Archers: hand weapon and bow; may have a buckler (+1 pt) and either javelins (+1 pt) or throwing or thrusting spear (+1 pt); Elite tribesmen may take light armour (+2 pts) and Kontarion & shield (+3 pts). Special rules: Avar models with light armour are Expert Horsemen and may use Feigned Flight; all other Avars are Nomad Cavalry. Armoured Nobles with Kontarion are Superior Cavalry. All Avar horsemen are Fearsome. All Subject units count as Levies unless they have at least one Avar model completely within 4" of their rear arc. Avars are immune to Panic caused by Subject infantry and cavalry fleeing or breaking. Slav, Bulgar and Gepid subjects cannot draw on the Leadership of the Avar Army General or the re-roll ability of the Avar Army Standard. Slavs in Avar armies do not benefit from the Balkan Ruse special rule.'),
 EK: (L(Characters=25, Infantry=20, Special_Units=25, **{'Allies_and_Mercenaries': 25}), 'EARLY KHAZARS (568-c.780 AD). An Army General and War Banner Bearer may be chosen (no other characters). Cavalry any amount; Infantry up to 20% if at least three Yurts have been taken as well (no infantry list is given for this tribe: Light Infantry is not available); Special Units up to 25% (Yurts and Defenders); Allies up to 25% on Subject Tribesmen and Alan cavalry (every other unit of Subject Tribesmen may be Riding Camels, +1 pt). Early Khazar Nobles equipment: hand weapon, bow and shield. May have light armour (+1 pt) and either javelins (+1 pt) or thrusting spear (+2 pts). Early Khazar Horse Archers: hand weapon, bow and shield; may have either javelins (+1 pt) or throwing spear (+1 pt). Early Khazar nobles with light armour are Expert Horsemen and may use Feigned Flight. All other Early Khazar cavalrymen are Nomad Cavalry.'),
 LK: (L(Characters=25, Infantry=33, Special_Units=25, **{'Allies_and_Mercenaries': 50}), 'LATER KHAZARS (c.780-1083 AD). Characters up to 25%; Cavalry any amount; Infantry up to 33% if at least three Yurts or the Beg War Wagon have been taken as well; Special Units up to 25% (either Yurts & Defenders, or the Beg War Wagon); Allies up to 50%: as Allied infantry Khazars may use two units of Northern Tribal Warriors (Rus list); as Allied cavalry, either 0-2 units of Alan and Magyar cavalry, or 0-3 units of Turkoman Cavalry (Late Byzantines list). Later Khazar Nobles equipment: hand weapon, light armour, bow and buckler. May have a shield (+1 pt), thrusting spear (+2 pts), heavy armour (+1 pt), and half-barding (+2 pts) or barding (+4 pts). Horse Archers: hand weapon, bow and buckler. May have either shield instead of buckler (free) or light armour (+1 pt); either javelins (+1 pt) or thrusting spear (+1 pt). Light Infantry: hand weapon and bow. May have a buckler (+1 pt), shield (+2 pts), throwing or thrusting spear (+1 pt) and light armour (+2 pts). Every other unit of Later Khazar Light Infantry may be Riding Camels (+1 pt). Later Khazar cavalrymen without armour are Nomad Cavalry. Later Khazar cavalry with light armour are Expert Horsemen and may use Feigned Flight. Later Khazar Noble Cavalry with heavy armour, spear and barding or half-barding are Superior Cavalry.'),
 MG: (L(Characters=25, Special_Units=25), 'MAGYARS (650-997 AD). An Army General and a War Banner Bearer may be chosen. Cavalry any amount; Infantry none; Special Units up to 25% (Yurts and Defenders); Allies: two units of Northern Tribal Warriors (Rus list). Magyar Nobles equipment: hand weapon and bow. May have light armour (+3 pts), buckler (+1 pt) and throwing spear (+2 pts). Magyar Horse Archers: hand weapon and bow. May have buckler (+1 pt) and throwing spear (+1 pt). Mounted Magyars are Nomad Cavalry and count as Fearsome, except to the Pechenegs who invaded their homelands in the 10th Century. Magyar Horse Archers fight as Skirmishers (not as Light Cavalry).'),
 PC: (L(Characters=25, Infantry=33, Special_Units=25), 'PECHENEGS (850-1122 AD). Characters up to 25%; Cavalry any amount; Infantry up to 33% on light infantry if at least three Wagons have been chosen as well; Special Units up to 25% (Wagons and Defenders). Pecheneg Nobles equipment: hand weapon, bow and shield. May have light armour (+3 pts) and either thrusting or throwing spear (+2 pts), or javelins (+1 pt). Horse Archers: hand weapon, bow and shield. May have either thrusting spear or throwing spear (+1 pt), or javelins (+1 pt). Light Infantry: hand weapon and bow. May have shield (+1 pt), and either javelins (+1 pt), or throwing spear (+1 pt). Mounted Pechenegs are Nomad Cavalry. Pecheneg Horse Archers and Light Infantry have a Leadership value of 6 and are subject to Warband Psychology. When formed, Pecheneg Horse Archers may count their rank bonus up to +2 but solely for Leadership purposes; they only count their rank bonus for close combat purposes in a turn they charge. To represent the massed charge of Pecheneg cavalry, formed units of Pecheneg horse archers and nobles fight as Massed Cavalry: they may count their rank bonus up to +2, but only if they charge. They do not count their rank bonus when they counter-charge. Massed Cavalry sacrifice mobility when they mass together - they lose half of their movement allowance if they wheel while marching or charging.'),
 CU: (L(Characters=25, Special_Units=25, **{'Allies_and_Mercenaries': 25}), 'EARLY CUMANS (1054-c.1200 AD). Characters up to 25% (Begs use the Lesser Khagan entry, Sultans the Khagan entry and Khans the Great Khagan entry); Cavalry any amount; Infantry none; Special Units up to 25% (Wagons and Defenders); Allies up to 25% on Subject Tribesmen and either Alan or Pecheneg cavalry; may also use any type of Balkan Cavalry, including Vlachs (Late Byzantines list - documented only). Cuman Nobles equipment: hand weapon, bow and buckler. May have shield (+1 pt), light armour (+3 pts), heavy armour (+3 pts) and throwing spear (+2 pts). Nobles with heavy armour may have thrusting or throwing spear (+2 pts). Cuman Horse Archers: hand weapon, bow and buckler. May have shield (+1 pt), light armour (+3 pts), and either throwing spear (+1 pt), or javelins (+1 pt). Cuman Wagon Defenders: hand weapon, javelins and shield. May exchange javelins for a bow (+1 pt). Every other Wagon Defender model may be equipped with a crossbow (+5 pts per model). Mounted Cumans are Nomad Cavalry, except Cumans with heavy armour, who are Expert Horsemen and have Feigned Flight. Cuman Horse Archers fight as Skirmishers (not as Light Cavalry).')})
ST_NOTES = ('STEPPE NOMADS (Byzantium: Beyond the Golden Gate, pp.55-60): Avars, Khazars, Magyars, Pechenegs and Cumans, 558-1200 AD. Instead of listing equipment options and special rules under each troop type, they are listed in the Army Composition of the chosen tribe as Nobles equipment and Horse Archers equipment. Characters may choose from the equipment available to the nobles of the selected tribe - note that the War Banner Bearer may not take spear, Kontarion or kontos. '
            'The options for each troop type here cover every tribe; options that belong to one tribe only are hidden unless that tribe is chosen. Options marked "included" are part of the tribe\'s standard equipment (free); take them if your tribe has them. Special rules: Mad Cavalry - tribesmen living most of their lives on horseback count as Nomad Cavalry (Expert Horsemen, Parthian Shot, Feigned Flight and Special Deployment).')
LISTS.append(('ST', 'Steppe Nomads (Byzantium)', 'Steppe_Nomads_Byzantium.cat', ST, STV, ST_NOTES))
